From: ASRock Rack X570D4I-2T OpenBMC port
Subject: [PATCH] read ahead and cache in front of the curl plugin
Upstream-Status: Pending

Un-filtered, every read the host issues through a Legacy (https://) mount
becomes its own HTTP range request over TLS. A BIOS enumerating and then
booting a device issues a great many small reads, so this is worth fixing on
its own merits: the two filters turn a sequential scan of an immutable image
into a handful of large fetches.

Filter order is load-bearing and reads backwards. nbdkit applies filters
client-first, so the leftmost sits on top; readahead must therefore be named
BEFORE cache to end up above it. readahead only does anything when the layer
below it reports NBDKIT_CACHE_NATIVE, and the curl plugin implements neither
.cache nor .can_cache -- so with the order reversed it logs "underlying plugin
does not support NBD_CMD_CACHE ... so the filter won't do anything" and
short-circuits, leaving every cache miss a serialised TLS round trip. Both man
pages document the correct order.

cache-on-read is needed because the mount is read-only and the cache filter
otherwise caches only writes. cache-max-size is deliberately small: the cache
is a temp file in $TMPDIR, which is tmpfs on this board, so it is unswappable
RAM on a 512 MiB BMC and is charged once per concurrent Legacy mount (two are
configured). With readahead correctly on top, the cache only has to hold the
prefetch window plus re-read of filesystem metadata.

NOT a fix for the POST instability seen on this board. With virtual media
attached the host intermittently loops between POST 0x60 and 0x77 and never
completes -- but that also happens with plain tmpfs-backed gadgets, and with
no gadget attached at all, so it is not caused by the latency this patch
addresses and this patch should not be credited with fixing it.

diff --git a/src/state/activating_state.cpp b/src/state/activating_state.cpp
index deb625f..0efbb27 100644
--- a/src/state/activating_state.cpp
+++ b/src/state/activating_state.cpp
@@ -263,6 +263,18 @@ std::unique_ptr<resource::Process>
 {
     std::unique_ptr<utils::VolatileFile> secret;
     std::vector<std::string> params = {
+        // Read ahead, then cache, in front of the plugin. Order matters and is
+        // the opposite of what it looks like: nbdkit applies filters
+        // client-first, so readahead must be named first to sit ON TOP of
+        // cache. readahead only works when the layer below reports
+        // NBDKIT_CACHE_NATIVE, which the curl plugin does not -- put it
+        // underneath and it logs "won't do anything" and short-circuits.
+        //
+        // Without them every block the host reads is its own HTTP range
+        // request over TLS. Reads are sequential and the image is immutable
+        // for the life of the mount, so this is safe and very effective.
+        "--filter=readahead",
+        "--filter=cache",
         // Use curl plugin ...
         "curl",
         // ... to mount http resource at url
@@ -274,7 +286,13 @@ std::unique_ptr<resource::Process>
         "ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384:"
         "ECDHE-ECDSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384",
         "tls13-ciphers="
-        "TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384"};
+        "TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384",
+        // cache-on-read because the mount is read-only and the cache filter
+        // otherwise caches only writes. The cache lives in $TMPDIR, which is
+        // tmpfs here, so cache-max-size is unswappable RAM on a 512 MiB BMC
+        // and is charged once per concurrent Legacy mount -- keep it small;
+        // readahead above only needs room for its prefetch window.
+        "cache-on-read=true", "cache-max-size=16M"};
 
     // Authenticate if needed.
     //
